1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Why should the second buyer pay more for
the same token?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
With Uniform Launch, each identity bids
once. Winners pay one price.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
On public BSC Testnet, our bidder
registered a stand-in Agent ID.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Our Factory created this Launcher.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
One bidder committed 4.45 test BNB.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
After close, anyone can settle.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
That call launches through Flap if the pool
qualifies.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Otherwise, bidders can claim full refunds.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,000
On this local nine-bid book, I set a
one-BNB budget and try two price limits.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,000
At the higher limit, my one BNB buys fifty
thousand tokens.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
I pay the shared clearing price shown here,
below my maximum.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Now I lower only my limit. The clearing
price stays at 0.00002.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
My bid gets zero tokens and the whole BNB
back.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Back on public testnet, that single bid
funded a real launch.

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Settlement opened a PancakeSwap V2 pool

16
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
with half a million UNIFORM and 4.45 test
BNB.

17
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Here is the fifth transaction. The bidder
claimed the other half million tokens.

18
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
You can verify that transfer on BscScan.

19
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
We built the Launcher interface using
Flap’s template.

20
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The public launch linked the Vault.

21
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:55,000
On a separate fork, trading fees reached it
and the owner withdrew them.

22
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Our code is public for both the Flap and
Developer tracks.
